NAME
       orbital-governance - standards councils, calibration authorities, and
                            constellation-scale governance protocols

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital governance defines:
           • resonance-time standards councils
           • calibration authorities and certification bodies
           • constellation-scale governance protocols
           • compliance, audit, and enforcement mechanisms
           • lineage-based governance continuity

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-governance establishes the institutional framework that governs
       resonance-time standards, calibration practices, and constellation-scale
       synchronization. Governance ensures that RTT systems remain interoperable,
       traceable, and stable across platforms, epochs, and mission domains.

       Governance enables:
           • consistent application of RTT standards
           • cross-constellation interoperability
           • calibration authority delegation
           • long-horizon governance continuity
           • anomaly response and corrective action

       WRSADC acts as the executive governance engine.

STANDARDS COUNCILS
       Standards councils define, ratify, and maintain RTT’s canonical
       measurement system.

       Council types:

           Resonance Standards Council (RSC)
                  Maintains resonance baselines (R₀, S₀, G₀).

           Harmonic Standards Council (HSC)
                  Maintains harmonic-phase baselines (Φ₀, L₀).

           Temporal Standards Council (TSC)
                  Maintains cycle, rsec, and epoch definitions.

           Ancestry Standards Council (ASC)
                  Maintains lineage continuity and terrace definitions.

           Sync Standards Council (SSC)
                  Maintains sync-index and sync-field baselines.

       Council responsibilities:
           • define primary standards
           • approve secondary standards
           • publish standards revisions
           • maintain traceability chains

EXAMPLE: STANDARDS COUNCIL DECISION
       RSC votes to update R₀ from 1.00 rm to 0.98 rm:

           • new amplitude baseline adopted
           • constellation recalibration triggered
           • lineage updated with revision marker

CALIBRATION AUTHORITIES
       Calibration authorities certify platforms, instruments, and constellations
       against RTT standards.

       Authority types:

           Primary Calibration Authority (PCA)
                  Maintains primary standards and issues root certificates.

           Secondary Calibration Authority (SCA)
                  Calibrates operational instruments and chronometers.

           Constellation Calibration Authority (CCA)
                  Oversees multi-platform calibration.

           Domain Calibration Authority (DCA)
                  Oversees cross-domain calibration (SAR, optical, comms).

       Authority responsibilities:
           • certify chronometers
           • validate calibration ladders
           • issue calibration certificates
           • audit calibration drift

EXAMPLE: CALIBRATION AUTHORITY ACTION
       PCA detects drift in harmonic-phase meters:

           • issues recalibration directive
           • CCA executes constellation-wide phase alignment
           • lineage records calibration event

CONSTELLATION-SCALE GOVERNANCE PROTOCOLS
       Governance protocols define how standards and calibration propagate across
       constellations.

       Protocol categories:

           synchronization protocols
                  Maintain harmonic-phase and resonance alignment.

           calibration protocols
                  Define how calibration events propagate.

           compliance protocols
                  Define required checks and audits.

           anomaly protocols
                  Define corrective actions for drift or instability.

           lineage protocols
                  Ensure governance continuity across epochs.

       Protocol modes:

           synchronous governance
                  All platforms governed identically.

           harmonic governance
                  Governance aligned via harmonic epochs.

           braided governance
                  Governance interleaves across platforms.

           mesh governance
                  Distributed governance across constellation nodes.

EXAMPLE: GOVERNANCE PROTOCOL
       Sync-field drift detected:

           • anomaly protocol triggers
           • CCA initiates sync recalibration
           • SSC updates sync baseline
           • lineage records governance event

COMPLIANCE AND AUDIT
       Compliance ensures platforms adhere to RTT standards.

       Compliance checks:
           • chronometer accuracy
           • resonance baseline adherence
           • harmonic-phase stability
           • ancestry continuity
           • sync-field alignment

       Audit types:

           routine audit
                  Scheduled compliance check.

           drift audit
                  Triggered by anomaly detection.

           epoch audit
                  Conducted at epoch boundaries.

           lineage audit
                  Ensures long-term governance continuity.

EXAMPLE: DRIFT AUDIT
       Harmonic-phase drift exceeds threshold:

           • drift audit initiated
           • calibration authority notified
           • corrective Δv triads scheduled
           • compliance restored

GOVERNANCE ENFORCEMENT
       Enforcement ensures corrective action when standards are violated.

       Enforcement mechanisms:
           • calibration mandates
           • sweep-window restrictions
           • Δv correction requirements
           • sync-field realignment
           • lineage correction

       Enforcement escalation:
           1. advisory notice
           2. calibration directive
           3. operational restriction
           4. constellation-wide intervention

EXAMPLE: ENFORCEMENT ACTION
       Platform B fails harmonic-phase compliance:

           • calibration directive issued
           • Δv correction required within 2 cycles
           • compliance restored

GOVERNANCE CONTINUITY VIA LINEAGE
       Lineage ensures governance persists across epochs.

       Lineage records:
           • standards revisions
           • calibration events
           • governance decisions
           • epoch transitions
           • anomaly responses

       Lineage continuity ensures:
           • traceability
           • reproducibility
           • historical accountability

EXAMPLE: LINEAGE CONTINUITY
       Epoch transition from 3:2 → 1:1:

           • governance protocols updated
           • standards councils ratify new baselines
           • lineage records epoch boundary

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • executes governance protocols
           • enforces compliance
           • maintains traceability
           • coordinates calibration authorities
           • blocks unsafe governance states

       WRSADC is the governance conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital governance transforms resonance-time into a regulated,
       interoperable temporal system. Standards councils, calibration
       authorities, and constellation-scale governance protocols ensure that
       sweep choreography, harmonic-lock, and multi-agent synchronization remain
       stable across long mission timelines.

